<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Food I Eat During a Celebration – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Festival Season.</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Food I Eat During a Celebration</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Use visual information in texts to convey meaning. Communication: Explain simple written instructions to peers, using English or Spanish.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="margin: 0; text-align: justify;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use visual information to clarify responses about everyday objects (e.g., points to cotton candy for "festival food"). Can explain simple written instructions to peers by pointing to pictures (e.g., points to a festival picture and says "festival"). Can describe festival traditions using gestures (e.g., clapping hands for "drums").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or realia of festival foods (e.g., cotton candy, shaved ice, corn on the cob) and festival elements (e.g., fireworks, drums, balloons). The teacher points to each item, saying the name, and students repeat in chorus. The teacher asks, "What do you like to eat at a festival?" and encourages students to point to their favorite food and say "I like [food]." The teacher leads TPR actions like clapping hands for "drums" or pretending to eat for "meal."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a simple, pre-made "Festival Storybook" page (Project 2 model) with a drawing of a festival food and a short sentence like "I like cotton candy. It is fun!" or "I eat corn on the cob." The teacher points to the picture and the words, clearly demonstrating how visuals convey meaning. The teacher models the pronunciation of /f/ in "fun" and /m/ in "meal," and the initial /p/ in "party," encouraging students to listen and repeat. The teacher then models explaining the page: "This is my storybook. I like cotton candy!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to a picture of cotton candy in the storybook model) "Is this festival food?" (Expected answer: "Yes!" or "Cotton candy!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to the sentence "I like cotton candy.") "Do I like this food?" (Expected answer: "Yes!" or "You like!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful "Festival Storybook" model with 2-3 pages. Each page features a clear illustration of a festival item or food (e.g., fireworks, shaved ice, dancing people) and a very simple descriptive sentence. For example: Page 1: Picture of fireworks. Text: "I like fireworks. They are bright!" Page 2: Picture of shaved ice. Text: "I eat shaved ice. It is yummy!" Page 3: Picture of kids dancing. Text: "I like music and dance. It is fun!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Using visual information in texts to convey meaning; Describing favorite festival parts using simple sentences and visual cues; Identifying initial sounds in festival-related words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows each page of the model storybook. For Page 1 (fireworks): "What do you see?" (Expected: "Fireworks!"). The teacher then points to the text "They are bright!" and asks, "Are fireworks bright?" (Expected: "Yes!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For Page 2 (shaved ice): "What food is this?" (Expected: "Shaved ice!"). The teacher then points to the sentence "It is yummy!" and asks, "Is it yummy?" (Expected: "Yes!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For Page 3 (music/dance): "What are they doing?" (Expected: "Dancing!" or "Singing!"). The teacher points to "fun" and asks, "Is it fun?" (Expected: "Yes!"). The teacher emphasizes the /m/ in music, /f/ in fun, and /p/ in party (if a picture of a party is included).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Subject + verb + adjective for descriptive sentences ("It is fun!", "They are bright!"). Present simple ("I like the meal.", "What do you like?"). Vocabulary: festival, celebration, party, fireworks, meal, shaved ice, cotton candy, corn on the cob, music, dance; like, love, eat, watch, celebrate, dance, sing, play, point, clap; fun, happy, loud, bright, colorful, big, special, exciting. Pronunciation: /f/ in "fireworks", /m/ in "meal", P /p/ in "party". Pragmatic: Describing favorite festival parts; Asking and answering about festival foods.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Visual-Text Matching (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides students with a worksheet containing pictures of various festival foods/activities (e.g., cotton candy, drums, fireworks) and separate word cards or short sentence cards (e.g., "Cotton candy", "It is loud!", "They are bright!"). Students match the picture to the correct word/sentence. The teacher walks around, providing support and checking accuracy.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Picture Description Chain (Semi-controlled):</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher starts by showing a picture of a festival food (e.g., corn on the cob) and saying "I like corn on the cob." The student next to the teacher points to another picture (e.g., music) and says "I like music. It is fun!" or "I hear music!" This continues around the circle, with each student using a simple sentence to describe a picture they choose. The teacher prompts with "What do you like?" if needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Storybook Page Explanation (Pair Interaction):</b> Students work in pairs with their *own* partially completed "Festival Storybook" pages (from previous lessons, which should have drawings or glued pictures). Student A points to a picture on their page (e.g., a drawing of shaved ice) and explains to Student B, "I like shaved ice." or "I eat shaved ice. It is yummy!" Student B then points to a picture on their page and does the same. They practice using visuals to convey their ideas about festival foods and activities. The teacher circulates, listening for use of target vocabulary and simple sentences, and offering assistance with pronunciation, especially /f/, /m/, and /p/.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Festival Storybook Show and Explain." Students will present one page of their individual "Festival Storybook" to a small group of peers (3-4 students). The core of this task is mediating meaning through visuals and simple verbal explanations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students sit in small groups. Each student takes turns presenting one page from their "Festival Storybook."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">When it's their turn, the student holds up their storybook page, points to the pictures they have drawn or pasted, and explains what they like about the festival food or activity using phrases like: "This is my festival page. I like [cotton candy]." "I eat [corn on the cob]. It is [yummy/big]!" or "I like [fireworks]. They are [bright]!" They can use gestures (e.g., clapping for drums, pretending to eat) to help convey meaning.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Peers in the group listen and can ask simple questions by pointing: "What is this?" or "Do you like [food]?" The presenting student clarifies by pointing back to their drawing or using simple English/Spanish as permitted by the objective.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing student interaction, communication strategies, and the use of visuals to mediate meaning. The teacher encourages all students to participate and offers prompts if needed ("Tell us what you like!"). This stage focuses on the confidence and ability to convey ideas using previously created visual texts.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist focusing on the mediation of meaning and use of target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student will present one page of their "Festival Storybook" to the teacher or a small group, clearly pointing to their drawings/pictures and explaining what they represent using at least two simple English sentences. They should use visuals to clarify their explanation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses visuals (points to pictures) to convey meaning.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., cotton candy, fireworks, fun, like, eat) intelligibly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses simple descriptive sentences (e.g., "I like it.", "It is fun!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to pronounce /f/ and /m/ sounds clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engages with peers/teacher during the explanation (maintains eye contact, responds to simple questions).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students will complete a short, visually supported exit ticket:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Emoji happy/sad face) "I used my pictures to tell about my festival food."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Check box) "I said 'I like...' or 'I eat...'"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy about explaining your storybook using pictures?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What festival food or activity do you want to learn more English words for?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this mediation lesson? Students were engaged when they used their own storybooks and pointed to their creations. The visual support was crucial for their explanations. What adjustments are needed for next time? Some students relied heavily on pointing without much verbal output; more explicit sentence frames for explaining visuals might be beneficial. Need to ensure consistent phonemic awareness practice, particularly for /f/ and /m/, by integrating simple rhymes or chants into warm-ups. The allowance of Spanish for clarification (as per objective) helped build confidence for some learners, but future lessons should aim to increase English dependency. Scaffolding for asking "What do you like?" and "What is it?" among peers could be strengthened.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "Food I Eat During a Celebration" theme. The next theme will build on descriptive language and cultural awareness by exploring "My Community and Traditions," where students will continue to use visuals to describe local events and practices, possibly creating visual guides or maps.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>